WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.283
Logs are where privacy problems collect.

2
00:00:02.563 --> 00:00:06.531
In the last lesson you decided what to record on every model call.

3
00:00:06.811 --> 00:00:15.579
This lesson is about the other side of that decision: what you must not record, what you record only in protected form, and how long anything stays.

4
00:00:15.859 --> 00:00:21.107
LLM systems make this harder than ordinary web services in a specific way.

5
00:00:21.387 --> 00:00:27.253
In a typical service, the sensitive data sits in a few known fields (a password, a card number).

6
00:00:27.533 --> 00:00:44.408
In an LLM system, the payload is free text written by users, and it flows everywhere: into prompts, into retrieved documents that get pasted into prompts, into tool arguments the model writes, and back out in the model’s answer, which can quote the user or a document.

7
00:00:44.688 --> 00:00:56.187
A customer who types their phone number into a support chat has put personal data into your prompt, your trace, your log, your eval set, and possibly your review queue, all in one request.

8
00:00:56.467 --> 00:01:05.064
So the question is not only “which fields are sensitive” but “which copies of user text exist, who can read each one, and when does each one die.

9
00:01:05.344 --> 00:01:10.933
” Every copy you make is a copy you must protect, disclose when asked, and delete on request.

10
00:01:11.213 --> 00:01:14.371
The cheapest copy to protect is the one you never made.
